UID:7add8c532af1d325@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261002T221213Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260629
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Chatrie v. United States
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 609 U. S. Part 2 \nPages 605–671 
 \nOFFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nJune 29\, 2026 \nREBECCA A. 
 WOMELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTICE
 : This preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound 
 volume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Decis
 ions\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \np
 io@supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \nPage 
 Proof Pending Publication\nOCTOBER TERM\, 2025 605 \nSyllabus \nCHATRIE v.
  UNITED STATES \ncertiorari to the united states court of appeals for \nth
 e fourth circuit \nNo. 25–112. Argued April 27\, 2026—Decided June 29\
 , 2026 \nOn May 20\, 2019\, a man robbed a credit union in Midlothian\, Vi
 rginia. \nLocal police offcers learned from witness interviews and surveil
 lance \nfootage that the robber had approached the credit union from a cor
 ner \nof an adjacent church while appearing to talk on a cell phone\, but 
 they \ncould not fnd out anything more\, and the robber remained at large.
  On \nJune 14\, the police offcers applied to a Virginia magistrate for a 
 geo-\nfence warrant directed to Google\, which would require Google to han
 d \nover data about the cell phones located within a 150-meter radius of t
 he \ncredit union—the so-called “geofence”—near the time of the cr
 ime. \nThe application described the cell-phone location data Google colle
 cts \nthrough a service called Location History\, which records the locati
 on of \na user's cell phone every two minutes or so. The application also 
 ex-\nplained how that cell-phone location data could help identify the rob
 ber\, \npossible accomplices\, or additional witnesses. The warrant descri
 bed a \nthree-step process that the police would follow: at step one\, Goo
 gle \nwould produce anonymized location data for all cell phones within th
 e \ngeofence 30 minutes before to 30 minutes after the robbery\; at step t
 wo\, \noffcers would attempt to narrow the list\, and Google would provide
  \nadditional an\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us2r64_7m
 io.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us2r64_7mio.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
